    "q-141235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141235",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$\n\nII. $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$\n\nIII. $(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3{x}^{3}}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3x}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
